    short: "The counting numbers $1, 2, 3, \\dots$, written $\\mathbb{N}$. Some books (and most programmers) also include $0$.",
    long: "Natural numbers are what you count with: one apple, two apples. They are closed under addition and multiplication (adding or multiplying two of them gives another), but not under subtraction ($3 - 5$) or division ($3 \\div 5$). Those gaps are exactly why the [[integer|integers]] and [[rational-number|rationals]] were invented.\n\nConventions differ on whether $0$ belongs: many math texts say $\\mathbb{N} = \\{1, 2, 3, \\dots\\}$ and call $\\{0, 1, 2, \\dots\\}$ the *whole numbers*; set theory and computer science usually start at $0$. When it matters, a careful author says which one is meant.\n\nIn code, natural numbers correspond to unsigned integer types such as `unsigned int` or `size_t`. They are bounded, so `0u - 1` wraps around to a huge value instead of becoming negative, a classic source of loop bugs.",
