    course: 'algebra',
    category: 'Numbers',
    short: "Any number that can be written as a fraction $\\tfrac{p}{q}$ of integers with $q \\ne 0$; the set is $\\mathbb{Q}$.",
    long: "Rational means *ratio*: $\\tfrac{3}{4}$, $-\\tfrac{5}{2}$, $7 = \\tfrac{7}{1}$ and $0.125 = \\tfrac{1}{8}$ are all rational. The rationals are closed under addition, subtraction, multiplication and division by a nonzero number, which makes them the first number system where every linear equation $ax = b$ with $a \\ne 0$ has a solution.\n\nIn decimal form a rational number either terminates ($0.375$) or eventually repeats ($0.\\overline{3} = \\tfrac{1}{3}$). Conversely, every terminating or repeating decimal is rational. Numbers whose decimals neither terminate nor repeat are [[irrational-number|irrational]].\n\nWhy it matters for programmers: a `double` stores a binary fraction, so most decimal rationals such as $0.1$ cannot be stored exactly. That is why `0.1 + 0.2 == 0.3` is false in C++, and why exact arithmetic libraries store numerators and denominators as integers.",
