    course: 'algebra',
    category: 'Numbers',
    short: "A real number that cannot be written as a ratio of integers; its decimal expansion never terminates and never repeats (e.g. $\\sqrt{2}$, $\\pi$, $e$).",
    long: "The diagonal of a unit square has length $\\sqrt{2}$, and the ancient Greeks proved that no fraction $\\tfrac{p}{q}$ squares to $2$. Numbers like this fill the gaps between the [[rational-number|rationals]] on the [[number-line|number line]]; together the two sets make up the [[real-number|real numbers]].\n\nThe square root of a whole number is either a whole number or irrational: $\\sqrt{9} = 3$, but $\\sqrt{2}, \\sqrt{3}, \\sqrt{5}, \\sqrt{8}$ are irrational. Other famous examples are $\\pi \\approx 3.14159$ and [[eulers-number|Euler's number]] $e \\approx 2.71828$.\n\nPitfall: a decimal approximation such as $\\pi \\approx 3.14$ or $\\sqrt{2} \\approx 1.414$ is *not* the number. Keep exact forms ($\\sqrt{2}$, $\\pi$) through a calculation and round only at the end.",
