    long: "Intuitively, absolute value strips the sign: $|5| = 5$ and $|-5| = 5$. Geometrically $|x|$ is the distance from $x$ to $0$, and $|a - b|$ is the distance between $a$ and $b$.\n\nPrecisely, $|x| = x$ when $x \\ge 0$ and $|x| = -x$ when $x < 0$. The second case looks odd but is right: if $x = -5$, then $-x = 5$. This is a [[piecewise-function|piecewise]] definition, and solving $|x - 3| = 4$ means splitting into the two cases $x - 3 = 4$ or $x - 3 = -4$.\n\nPitfalls: $|a + b| \\ne |a| + |b|$ in general (try $a = 3, b = -3$), and $\\sqrt{x^2} = |x|$, not $x$. In C++ use `std::abs` for integers and `std::fabs` or `std::abs` from `<cmath>` for doubles.",
