    short: "The agreed order for evaluating an expression: grouping, then exponents, then multiplication and division (left to right), then addition and subtraction (left to right).",
    long: "Without a convention, $2 + 3 \\cdot 4$ could mean $20$ or $14$. Everyone agrees it is $14$: multiplication binds tighter than addition. The mnemonic PEMDAS lists the levels: Parentheses, Exponents, Multiplication/Division, Addition/Subtraction.\n\nThe crucial detail is that M and D share one level and are done left to right, and so are A and S. So $8 \\div 2 \\cdot 4 = 4 \\cdot 4 = 16$, not $1$, and $10 - 3 + 2 = 9$, not $5$. Fraction bars and radicals act as grouping symbols.\n\nThe classic trap is $-3^2$: the exponent applies before the negation, so $-3^2 = -9$ while $(-3)^2 = 9$. Programming languages encode the same idea as operator precedence; note that C++ has no exponent operator, and `^` means XOR.",
