    long: "Adding $0$ or multiplying by $1$ changes nothing, and that is surprisingly useful. Multiplying by a clever form of $1$, such as $\\tfrac{x}{x}$ or $\\tfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{3}}$, is how you get a [[least-common-denominator|common denominator]] or perform [[rationalizing-the-denominator|rationalizing the denominator]]. Adding a clever form of $0$, like $+9 - 9$, is the heart of [[completing-the-square|completing the square]].\n\nIdentities pair with inverses: the additive inverse of $a$ is $-a$ (since $a + (-a) = 0$), and the multiplicative inverse is the [[reciprocal|reciprocal]] $\\tfrac{1}{a}$ (since $a \\cdot \\tfrac{1}{a} = 1$).\n\nThe idea generalizes: the identity function $f(x) = x$ is the identity for [[composition|composition]], and $f(f^{-1}(x)) = x$ says an [[inverse-function|inverse function]] undoes $f$.",
