    long: "Solving an equation is mostly applying inverse operations to both sides, in the reverse order that the operations were applied to $x$. In $3x + 5 = 20$, $x$ was multiplied by $3$ and then $5$ was added, so you subtract $5$ first and then divide by $3$.\n\nThe pairs you will use throughout algebra: $+a$ and $-a$; $\\times a$ and $\\div a$ (for $a \\ne 0$); $x^n$ and $\\sqrt[n]{x}$; $b^x$ and $\\log_b x$. Each pair is a small example of an [[inverse-function|inverse function]].\n\nPitfall: some inverses only work on a restricted domain. Squaring is undone by $\\sqrt{\\ }$ only for $x \\ge 0$; in general $\\sqrt{x^2} = |x|$. That is why squaring both sides of an equation can create an [[extraneous-solution|extraneous solution]].",
