    long: "In $5^3$ the base is $5$ and the [[exponent|exponent]] is $3$. The exponent attaches only to what is immediately to its left: in $2x^3$ the base is $x$, not $2x$, and in $-4^2$ the base is $4$, not $-4$. Parentheses change the base: $(2x)^3 = 8x^3$.\n\nThe word also appears in [[exponential-function|exponential functions]] $f(x) = b^x$ (with $b > 0$, $b \\ne 1$) and in [[logarithm|logarithms]] $\\log_b x$, where it is the same number: $\\log_b x$ asks which power of the base gives $x$. Common bases are $10$, $2$ (the base of binary) and [[eulers-number|Euler's number]] $e$.\n\nThe laws of exponents only combine powers with the *same* base: $2^3 \\cdot 2^4 = 2^7$, but $2^3 \\cdot 3^4$ cannot be simplified that way.",
