    long: "Very large and very small numbers are hard to read as strings of zeros. Scientific notation separates the significant digits ($a$) from the scale ($10^n$): the speed of light is about $3.0 \\times 10^8$ m/s and a hydrogen atom is about $1.1 \\times 10^{-10}$ m wide.\n\nThe laws of [[exponent|exponents]] make arithmetic easy: $(3 \\times 10^4)(2 \\times 10^5) = 6 \\times 10^9$. After multiplying or dividing, renormalize so the leading factor is between $1$ and $10$: $25 \\times 10^3 = 2.5 \\times 10^4$.\n\nIn code this is E notation: `6.02e23` means $6.02 \\times 10^{23}$ and `1e-9` means $10^{-9}$. Floating-point numbers are stored the same way, but in base 2, which is why their *relative* precision is constant no matter the magnitude.",
